FRAMEBUFFER / LED MATRIX CONTROL AND VERIFICATION

Current fbctl.c commands:
  fbctl status      read /proc/fb
  fbctl on          enable matrix framebuffer
  fbctl off         disable and release GPIO resources
  fbctl clear       clear framebuffer
  fbctl test        blink heart three times

The 8x8 LED matrix uses GPIO resources also used by display demos.
Beware simultaneous use by fbctl, scroll and other programs.

READ-ONLY START
  pins
  fbctl status
  help fbctl

ILLUSTRATIVE CONTROL (explicitly approved only)
  fbctl on
  fbctl clear
  fbctl status
  fbctl off
'clear' and 'off' change display state; they are not read-only.
The test animation is intentionally visible and changes the LEDs.

SCROLL
  echo HELLO | scroll
This finite stdin pipeline is preferable to 'scroll -u 2001',
which starts a long-running UDP listener. A foreground scrolling
operation may still exceed MCP's 3-second command timeout.
Avoid starting long-running UI or server programs through MCP.

TROUBLESHOOTING
  No LEDs: examine pins, /proc/fb, board orientation/polarity,
  framebuffer ownership and physical power/wiring.
  Unexpected patterns: check whether a prior demo is active.
  Do not treat successful command exit as proof of optical output.


FBCHAR fixed-display device (/dev/fbchar)
----------------------------------------
Write exactly one printable ASCII character (32..126), or one of the
case-sensitive pattern names: heart, star, smile, sad, up, down, left,
right, check, cross, clear. The command `echo A > /dev/fbchar` displays
A; `echo heart > /dev/fbchar` draws a heart; `echo clear > /dev/fbchar`
clears the 8x8 matrix. The display persists after close(). The device rejects unknown names and does not scroll text.
Read with `cat /dev/fbchar` to list available patterns and usage;
the listing is finite and does not change the display.
Unlike /dev/fb (8 binary bytes), /dev/fbchar accepts ASCII.
Do not run scroll or other framebuffer writers concurrently. Python
may open the device for writing and call write(fd, "heart").
